# RTB: templates first — do not change runtime

## Critical rule (hard stop)

**Never change runtime** (`playwright-runtime` wrap/sanitize/syntax fallback, Browserless call shape, or anything that rewrites how saved scripts execute) to fix generation or new scenarios.

Reason: shared DB + AWS users keep **previously generated working scripts**. A runtime change can break those overnight even when the script text never changed.

Work only on **working templates** (assemble + references + detectors). New patterns = new/saved templates, not runtime patches.

## Template strategy

~80% of every script is the same: login, navigate, apply filters, waits. Only the **extract** part differs.

| User / scenario intent | Use template |
|------------------------|--------------|
| KPI / Overview tiles | KPI / `overview_kpi` (or activity KPI) |
| Graph / chart / Show Data | Chart / `chart_show_data` |
| Grid / crosstab / Geography | Grid / `geography_grid` |

When generating: pick the template from the scenario description; fill URL, `NAV_STEPS`, labels, time grain, filter keys from UI — do not invent screens.

## Saving new working scripts as templates

When the user provides a **working** Playwright script for a new scenario pattern:

1. Capture it as a **reference / template** under the skill pipeline (`_shared/references/`, assemble helpers, detectors) — not as a one-off runtime hack.
2. Wire classify → assemble so the **same pattern** reuses that template next time.
3. Keep shared login/nav/filter mechanics stable across templates; only specialize extract.

## Allowed vs forbidden

- **Allowed:** template JS, assemblers, nav/title parse, generate-time validation expectations, skill docs.
- **Forbidden without explicit ask:** changes to `playwright-runtime` wrap/sanitize/syntax fallback that alter how existing saved scripts run.
